5. @Valid vs @Validated
--------------------------------------------------

@Valid      -> Jakarta standard (jakarta.validation.Valid)
@Validated  -> Spring (org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated)

    Feature                        @Valid    @Validated
    Validation groups              No        Yes
    Can be placed on a class       No        Yes
    Cascades into nested objects   Yes       No

Usage:
  - Request body validation:  @Valid @RequestBody CategoryRequest request
  - Validation groups:        @Validated(OnCreate.class)
  - @PathVariable / @RequestParam validation: put @Validated on the class
  - Nested objects: put @Valid on the nested field

Controller example:

@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<CategoryResponse> createCategory(
        @Valid @RequestBody CategoryRequest request) {
    CategoryResponse response = categoryService.createCategory(request);
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(response);
}


6. IMPORTANT NOTES
--------------------------------------------------

- Validation does nothing unless the DTO has constraints AND the
  controller parameter has @Valid or @Validated.
- Most constraints (@Size, @Email, @Pattern, @Min) treat null as valid.
  Add @NotNull or @NotBlank too if the field is required.
- On failure, Spring throws MethodArgumentNotValidException -> 400 Bad Request.
- Bean validation checks FORMAT only. Business rules (e.g. "name already
  exists") belong in the service layer.
- Keep database constraints (NOT NULL, UNIQUE) as the last line of defense.
- Match @Size limits with your @Column(length = ...) on the entity.
- Hibernate Validator extras: @Length, @Range, @URL, @CreditCardNumber
  (some deprecated; prefer standard ones when available).
- Custom constraints: write your own with @Constraint and a
  ConstraintValidator (e.g. @UniqueCategoryName).


7. QUICK REFERENCE - ERRORS SEEN EARLIER
--------------------------------------------------

Error: Cannot make a static reference to the non-static method
       createCategory(CategoryRequest) from the type CategoryService
Cause: Calling CategoryService.createCategory(...) on the class.
Fix:   Inject the service and call it on the instance:
       categoryService.createCategory(request);


8. JACKSON (JSON <-> Java)
--------------------------------------------------

- Deserialization: JSON -> Java object (used by @RequestBody)
- Serialization:   Java object -> JSON (used by ResponseEntity body)
- Already included via spring-boot-starter-web (no separate dependency).
- DTO needs a default constructor plus setters (or Lombok @Data/@Getter/@Setter).
- Useful annotations: @JsonProperty, @JsonIgnore, @JsonInclude(NON_NULL)